Metalation of NacNac ligands is typically achieved by treatment with an alkyl lithium followed by the addition of a metal salt. However, this procedure can deprotonate the SBUs of I potentially resulting in multiple catalytically active sites in the MOF. Instead, we first prepared metal complexes of the neutral NacNac ligand and then deprotonated the NacNac ligand to afford the desired metal complexes of the monoanionic NacNac ligand (Figure 1c). Solutions of \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}\bullet4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), and \([\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{MeCN})_{4}]\mathrm{PF}_{6}\) in THF were added to I to give MOF-immobilized neutral NacNac complexes \((\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{I}){\bullet}\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\), (H\(\mathrm{I}){\bullet}\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}\), and \([(H{-}I){\bullet}\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{MeCN})_{2}]\mathrm{PF}_{6}\). Inductively coupled plasma-mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) of digested metalated MOFs showed that the extents of metalation were \(60\%\), \(98\%\), and \(80\%\) for Co, Fe, and Cu (with respect to the NacNac ligand in I), respectively. Deprotonation and subsequent functionalization of the metals were then performed to afford the metal complexes of the monoanionic NacNac ligand in I. \((\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{I}){\bullet}\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}\) was treated with 5 equiv of MeLi to afford I•Fe(Me), whereas (H\(\mathrm{I}){\bullet}\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\) was treated with 10 equiv of \(\mathrm{NaBHEt}_{3}\) to give I•Co(H). \([(H{-}I){\bullet}\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{MeCN})_{2}]\mathrm{PF}_{6}\) was deprotonated with addition of 1.5 equiv KOtBu in THF to give the desired I•Cu(THF) species. A similar neutral metalation protocol was employed to deprotonate preformed \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\) complexes

of a family of P-N-P ligands.14 PXRD patterns of the metalated MOF samples showed that MOFs maintain crystallinity after metalation processes.

N,N'-Ph2-NacNac (H-L1) was used to prepare homogeneous controls in order to obtain the coordination environments of the M-NacNac complexes in I and to compare the performance of MOF catalysts with the homogeneous control catalysts. Single crystals of \((\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{Li}){\bullet}\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\) and \((\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}\mathbf{1}){\bullet}\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}\) were prepared from complexation of the neutral ligand with metal salts followed by crystallization in concentrated solutions of THF or ether at -10 \(^{\circ}C\), respectively (SI). Attempts to prepare complexes of \((\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{Li}){\bullet}\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{MeCN})_{2}\) however failed due to immediate precipitation of amorphous powders under various conditions. For the preparation of homogeneous catalysts, precursors \(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{\bullet}\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}\) and \(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{\bullet}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}\) were prepared from deprotonation of (H-L1) followed by addition of either \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}(\mathrm{THF})_{1.5}\) or \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}\) in THF. These species were then treated with either MeLi or NaBHEt to afford the active catalyst \(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{\bullet}\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e})\) or \(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{\bullet}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}(\mathbf{H})\), respectively. Similar functionalization strategies of NacNac derivatives have been previously reported by Holland and coworkers.15 The corresponding homogeneous \(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{\bullet}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}\) catalyst was prepared by addition of CuOtBu to a solution of (H-L1) in benzene.
