We also examined the steric and electronic influences of substituted anilines in intermolecular C-H amination reactions. In agreement with the trends observed from Warren's previous reports, homogeneous catalyst \(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{I}^{\bullet}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}\) gives higher TONs for the amination with sterically congested 2,4,6-substituted anilines (Table 3, entry 8 and 10). In contrast, \(\mathbf{I}^{\bullet}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}(\mathrm{THF})\) afforded lower yields of amination products for the tri-substituted anilines 3b and 3c.

We followed the reaction progress for the amination of cyclohexene with either aniline (3a) or 2,4,6-trimethylaniline (3b) by GC-MS over 9 days at a reduced catalyst loading of \(0.5\ \mathrm{mol\%}\) (Figure 4). For smaller aniline 3a, the homogeneous catalyst initially gave higher conversion than the MOF. However, the conversion afforded by \(\mathbf{I}^{\bullet}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}(\mathrm{THF})\) increased linearly while the homogeneous catalyst's conversion slowed after 3 days, suggesting the decomposition of the homogeneous catalyst via biomolecular deactivation. In contrast, \(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{I}^{\bullet}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}\) afforded significantly higher conversions for the sterically hindered 2,4,6-trimethylaniline 3b than \(\mathbf{I}^{\bullet}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}(\mathrm{THF})\) throughout the course of the reaction, indicating the strong influence of substrate sizes due to different substrate diffusion rates. Interestingly, while \(2\ \mathrm{mol\%}\) of \(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{I}^{\bullet}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}\) catalyzed the reaction of sterically demanding 1,2,3,4-tetrahydronapthalene with aniline 3a affording the amination product 5a in \(100\%\) NMR yield, the MOF catalyst gave no conversion to the desired product. These results show that the MOF acts as a size-selective catalyst for the intermolecular amination reaction.

