# Catalytic Hydrogenation of Alkenes

Hydrogenation catalysts are widely used for the industrial production of commodity and fine chemicals and for the conversion of biomass to biorenewable fuels.33 Historically, hydrogenation reactions are catalyzed using precious metal catalysts, but their replacement with earth-abundant first row metals is of increasing importance due to cost and availability. Homogeneous cobalt phosphine- and pyridine-based catalysts have been identified for the hydrogenation of alkenes.34 Additionally, we recently reported a remarkable example where a homogeneous Fe-salicylaldimine complex was an inactive hydrogenation catalyst but functionalization within a MOF leads to a highly active catalyst for the hydrogenation of alkenes.4 We report here the first demonstration of catalytic hydrogenation with a CoNacNac complex and its MOF analog.

We surveyed the catalytic activities of Co-NacNac MOFs in the hydrogenation of 1-octene. At \(0.0005\ \mathrm{mol\%}\) catalyst loading, \(\mathbf{I}\mathbf{\bullet}\mathbf{Co}(\mathrm{H})\) catalyzes the hydrogenation of 1-octene to afford \(n\) -octane in \(100\%\) yield in 1.5 days (Table 4, entry 1). ICP-MS studies showed that \(Z\mathbf{r}\) and Co were not detectable in the supernatants. For comparison, at \(0.0005\ \mathrm{mol\%}\) loading, homogeneous \(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{\bullet}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}(\mathrm{H})\) affords

\(65\%\) yield of \(n\) -octane (Table 4, entry 2) However, \(\mathbf{I}\mathbf{\bullet}\mathbf{Fe}(\mathrm{H})\) was significantly less active, affording only \(10\%\) n-octane at \(0.1\;\mathrm{mol}\%\) catalyst loading (Table 4, entry 3). This result contrasts our earlier observation with the Fesalicylaldimine MOF which was highly active for hydrogenation of 1-octene.4 To rule out the participation of Co nanoparticles, at \(0.0005\ \mathrm{mol\%}\) Co, only \(6\%\) conversion was observed (Table 4, entry 4). When catalyst loadings were lowered to \(0.0001\ \mathrm{mol\%}\) , \(\mathbf{I}\mathbf{\bullet}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}(\mathrm{H})\) afforded 1-octane in \(70\%\) yield leading to a TON of 700,000 (Table 4, entry 5), whereas \(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{\bullet}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}(\mathrm{H})\) gave no conversion (Table 4, entry 6).

\(\mathbf{I}\mathbf{\bullet}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}(\mathrm{H})\) was found to catalyze a broad scope of alkenes. Styrene (6b) was quantitatively hydrogenated at \(0.0005\ \mathrm{mol\%}\) of \(\mathbf{I}\mathbf{\bullet}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}(\mathrm{H})\) (Table 4, entry 7). Cyclohexene was hydrogenated in quantitative yield at \(0.025\ \mathrm{mol\%}\) of \(\mathbf{I}\mathbf{\bullet}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}(\mathrm{H})\) in 3 days (Table 4, entry 8). Additionally, \(0.025\ \mathrm{mol\%}\) of \(\mathbf{I}\mathbf{\bullet}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}(\mathrm{H})\) catalyzed hydrogenation of 4-methoxyallylbenzene (6d) in \(79\%\) yield after 2 days (Table 4, entry 9).

